<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Masovia</h1><p class="st-meta">1138 CE – 1526 CE · capital Plock</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The long-lived duchy of central Poland whose duke invited the Teutonic Knights and whose town of Warsaw became Poland's capital.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a>, on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> with its centre at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-plock">Plock</a>, was given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iv-the-curly">Boleslaw IV the Curly</a> under the testament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Boleslaw III</a> in 1138. From 1199 it was held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-i-of-masovia">Konrad I</a> and his descendants, a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piasts</a> that ruled it for over three centuries.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Exposed to raids by the pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-prussians">Prussians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-i-of-masovia">Konrad I</a> invited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> in 1226 and gave it the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-land">Chelmno</a>, a decision with lasting consequences. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a> was a poor, heavily forested land with a large petty nobility. Its dukes often divided it into smaller duchies such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-plock">Plock</a> and Rawa, and from 1351 they were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the Polish king.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janusz-i-of-warsaw">Janusz I</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> his main seat in the early 15th century. When the last dukes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stanislaw-of-masovia">Stanislaw</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janusz-iii-of-masovia">Janusz III</a>, died young in 1524 and 1526, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a> was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-poland-1025-1385">Kingdom of Poland</a> (1385-1569), and Warsaw became the royal capital in 1596.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-masovia.jpg" alt="Duchy of Masovia" width="320" height="198"><figcaption>Image: Poznaniak · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chor%C4%85giew_ksi%C4%99cia_mazowieckiego_Siemowita.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1138 CE – 1526 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Plock</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Konrad I of Masovia, Janusz I, Janusz III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mazovia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1332&amp;focus=duchy-of-masovia" aria-label="Duchy of Masovia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5122 365 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Masovia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5442 621L5457 692M5442 621L5457 692M5442 621L5457 643M5442 621L5409 614M5442 621L5432 635"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5457 692h0M5457 692h0M5457 643h0M5409 614h0M5432 635h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5442 621h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5442 621h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1332&amp;focus=duchy-of-masovia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
